<p>Since I haven't been on the forums in a while and this grabbed my attention I guess I'm gonna take a crack at this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The Tests(not in the idea critique) can be cross-referenced with the statements from the SCP to uncover what happened. Statements of |||||||||| is text that has been scratched out beyond recognition</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not exactly sure what you mean by this? Are basically saying that although what happens won't be outright stated, it can be solved if you just pay a bit more attention? That isn't a page format, it's probably just something that should go in the elevator pitch or central narrative. I have no idea what to say about the |||||||||||| part. Are you trying to talk about a redaction? You can just say that you don't need to show it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dealing with retro-causality and how to test an anomalous object that can change the past.</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Testers have issues discovering how the SCP functions since it wipes people's minds and rewrites history and records the instance of the change.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is almost the whole idea of SCP-140, a book about the Daevites that rewrites history and details this in the book whenever it's given blood or something (I honestly don't remember), not just in the book but also in our real world. Although people's minds aren't wiped they don't take notice, until they notice it. Not to mention there are countless time-space-changing-cognitohazard books out on the mainlist. So your base concept has already been done. Also, this elevator pitch is hella short lmao.</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Book was discovered to have been placed into SCP containment by someone who might have known the book's anomalous properties.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>Alright. But how was this placed into containment? Did they just plop it into an empty containment cell? There aren't just spare cells up for use on the daily when someone does a flick of the wrist. How could this person have even put it in containment? Who is this person? Are they even central to the anomaly or are they just there to conveniently drop this good old book off for reading at their nearest site? Does this even add anything to the anomaly? Shouldn't this be in the elevator pitch??</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Unfortunate Accident causes it to end up in the research department</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Unfortunate Accident causes it to end up in the research department&quot;.. Okay. I'm not even gonna ask what that even means because this still literally tells me nothing about the anomaly or the concept. Do you think the Foundation just let these things sit in their neat containment cell while the security guards and researchers sit around and eat pringles all day? Isn't it standard that these anomalies end up in the research department regardless of anything?</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Marion Wheeler discovers and returns the book back to containment.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh my god. Hold the phone bro. Marion Wheeler? Why is she even here? I just scrolled down and you don't even mention her again, what is she even adding to this. You know what, alright. How would she have discovered the book if the book was already in containment?? Why would she need to return the book to containment, isn't it already in containment, why would researchers even have to take it out of containment..????? Shouldn't all of this be in the elevator pitch as well..</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Testing begins</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>OK.</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Containment Breach by a D-Class individual using the book during a test.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>Didn't you just say that the book wipes the minds of everyone that reads it? How would the D-Class even use the book, it changes the past, not the present, so it can't be used to blow a hole to freedom, is this gonna be presented as an elaborate scheme where the D places something to help him escape in the present in the past with the book? It's not like the D is gonna have access to the book at all times, it's not even guaranteed that a D-Class works with an anomaly more than once. Can it just be influenced to change the past on the fly?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Content Summary-</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;.Dude the hook/attention grabber section isn't used for this. This should all be in either the elevator pitch or central narrative, even the format of this is disjointed and jumbled all over the place. <strong>THIS WHOLE SECTION DOESN'T EVEN TELL ANYONE WHY THEY SHOULD BE INTERESTED IN THIS.</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>While within 5 meters of SCP-####. Do not speak or write any untrue statements. In an emergency, you can talk in future tense but you must not make any statement about the past or present (specifically opinions or statements that are false or have the possibility of being false)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The 5 meters tells us nothing about the anomaly, in fact, most of what I've already nitpicked doesn't even elaborate on what the anomaly does beyond being a book that changes the past, and if it's just that I already told you the concept has already been done before.</p>
<p>In an emergency? What does talking about future tense even do? Again you've never elaborated on any of this. Now the book can change shit about the present. Man, how are you gonna contradict your idea and change it mid-way through. The reason I've been so harsh with this is because some of these sentences make NO SENSE because you haven't given context where it should and the fact that you have this concept and format all over the place. How can an opinion be false?? What does any of this mean??????</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The text of SCP-#### is a range of statements about our world. More recent statements have been able to be verified as true. So far, no statements have been verified as false.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This just raises more questions for me, if none of the statements are false then wouldn't the book would just be considered to be a book with weird stuff in it? How would the Foundation even detect the mind-erasing and history-changing? Btw you can just refer to the SCP as SCP-XXXX and not ####.</p>
<p>I'm not gonna do a line by line for the rest because I'm gonna drive myself mad. Also, chill with the redactions and black-boxing, it's not intriguing and it'll probably just make people feel like they're gonna have a stroke reading that or not make anything out at all. Also the way you're describing the &quot;Containment Summary&quot; and &quot;Description Summary&quot; (which summary is literally a synonym for description) just makes it look like you separated two things that should probably be put in one paragraph for no reason.</p>
<p>Like I said, I have no words for the rest cause it's legit just random text. You bulleted almost everything when you should only bullet things in the elevator pitch. I'm sorry if it seemed that I was a bit rude and rough when it came to the crit but I just want to be brutally honest. As it stands I don't think the idea would make it on the mainlist and if it did it would probably just go into deletion. I hope you come up with some more ideas because even if you don't always think up bangers instantly I do think every author deserves a chance.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

